    "ym-design-regulator-atlas",
    "Generate and audit a regulator atlas for constructive Yang--Mills theory without privileging prior finite models. Use when comparing Wilson, heat-kernel, Villain-type, Hamiltonian, flowed-observable, or multiscale regulators for gauge invariance, reflection positivity, locality, RG closure, topology, observable renormalization, and continuum universality.",
    "---\nname: ym-design-regulator-atlas\ndescription: Generate and audit a regulator atlas for constructive Yang--Mills theory without privileging prior finite models. Use when comparing Wilson, heat-kernel, Villain-type, Hamiltonian, flowed-observable, or multiscale regulators for gauge invariance, reflection positivity, locality, RG closure, topology, observable renormalization, and continuum universality.\n---\n\n## Abducer Integration Guards\n\n- Activate this skill only for `yang-mills-existence-and-mass-gap-target` or an explicitly marked Yang-Mills calibration task.\n- Treat imported representational-dynamics artifacts as restricted, finite, conditional, or support evidence unless a later proof discharges their frontier hypotheses.\n- Emit claim level, non-claims, hard gate, hypotheses, falsifier, and demotion trigger before any promotion.\n- Compose with the target-neutral proof-depth skills: pressure formalization, hard-gate selection, nontriviality audit, anti-definitionality audit, same-domain transfer certification, and declarative learning extraction.\n- State efficient representation requirements: preserved observables, morphisms or recovery maps, invariants, error budgets, equivalence policy, locality or cutoff budget, transfer interface, and falsifiers.\n- Do not let a transfer theorem pass if the target property is built into the bridge definition; require independently stated source and target objects plus a consequence delta.\n- Preserve the four-cycle, recursion-depth-one publication-consolidation limit for local subgoals.\n- Source provenance: `agentic/external/yang_mills_representational_dynamics/2026-07-20/skill_bundle/11_vscode_codex_skill_bundle/.agents/skills/ym-design-regulator-atlas/SKILL.md`.\n\n## Mathematical Invariants\n\nPreserve the Yang--Mills mathematical object: compact simple gauge group, pure gauge content, gauge-invariant observable algebra, regulator/cutoff/volume/coupling regime, reflection positivity, locality, OS reconstruction route, continuum limit order, and Hamiltonian spectral target. Any representation, morphism, RG step, or restricted model must state which invariants are preserved, which error budgets are allowed, and which edge case or counterexample would break transfer.\n\n## Epistemic Discipline\n\nEvery output must state epistemic status, claim level, evidence citation, source artifacts, explicit unknowns, and validation handoff. Treat physical relevance, literature-gap warrant, and frontier comparison as hard gates; unsupported implications stay conditional, blocked, or `requires_prior_art`.\n\n## Failure/Demotion\n\nDemote or quarantine if the skill assumes a mass gap, clustering, confinement, continuum existence, regulator independence, positivity, locality, observable density, or theory identity instead of proving it. Disconfirming evidence, prior-art collision, target-equivalent bridge definitions, or missing counterexample tests block promotion to a major YM publication pathway.\n\n# Design a Yang--Mills Regulator Atlas\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Freeze the continuum target and observable core before scoring regulators.\n2. Specify each candidate's carrier, action/measure, gauge action, positivity structure, boundary conditions, and parameters.\n3. Test exact gauge invariance, locality, reflection positivity, transfer construction, and finite-volume well-definedness.\n4. Test closure or controlled enlargement under block RG.\n5. Audit small-field coordinates, large fields, topological sectors, and gauge-orbit coverage.\n6. State renormalization requirements for local composites and extended observables.\n7. Separate proved universality from expected universality.\n8. Rank candidates by the severity of missing bridges to a nontrivial OS-positive continuum theory.\n9. Select a primary regulator and an adversarial alternative; justify the choice with theorem obligations.\n\n## Comparison matrix\n\nAlways include: exact invariances; positivity; locality; RG compatibility; large-field control; topology; continuum scaling; observable completeness; known rigorous regime; failure modes; and claim status.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Do not select a regulator because prior work already uses it.\n- Do not assume regulator independence; formulate a universality theorem target.\n- Reject regulators whose positivity loss has no recovery route.\n- Mark strong-coupling and weak-coupling regimes separately.\n- Treat dual or extra-dimensional regulators as changes of theory until exact equivalence is proved.\n\n## Output contract\n\nReturn the atlas, evidence-backed ranking, selected primary and adversarial regulators, required universality theorem, and exact reasons any candidate is excluded.\n\n## Packaged PRISM Output Contract\n\nReturn exactly the generated `BoundedDomainFrontier` JSON object. Do not paraphrase closed variant values.\n\n- Follow the exact generated JSON schema without extra fields.\n",
    "{\"agents/openai.yaml\":\"\"}",
)
